{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/a2-everyday-customs-france-usa-greetings/audio/main-083942beb0fc.mp3","durationSec":309.912,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":2.06,"text":"Imagine one French woman on one day."},{"start":2.62,"end":6.36,"text":"In the morning, she meets a new client at the office, and they shake hands."},{"start":7.06,"end":14.22,"text":"In the evening, she arrives at her sister's birthday dinner, and she greets everyone with la bise, the cheek kiss from the last lesson."},{"start":14.98,"end":18.66,"text":"She is the same person, but she uses two different greetings."},{"start":19.48,"end":19.98,"text":"What changed?"},{"start":20.82,"end":21.9,"text":"Only the relationship."},{"start":22.74,"end":29.74,"text":"In the USA, people often say \"Hello\" and shake hands, especially in professional places like an office."},{"start":30.52,"end":32.74,"text":"Some people do not feel comfortable with a handshake."},{"start":33.5,"end":37.8,"text":"For them, there is a polite choice: they can put a hand on their chest and bow a little."},{"start":38.62,"end":41.54,"text":"You may remember the bows from Japan in the last lesson."},{"start":42.3,"end":45.3,"text":"Here, a small bow is not the main custom."},{"start":45.96,"end":48.78,"text":"It is a polite way to say hello without touching."},{"start":50,"end":54.58,"text":"American manners also change from place to place and from one situation to another."},{"start":55.08,"end":57.44,"text":"This is also true when you visit an American home."},{"start":58.08,"end":61.66,"text":"Some families take off their shoes at the door, and some do not."},{"start":62.4,"end":67.08,"text":"You remember the slippers in Japan, but there is not one rule for every American home."},{"start":67.9,"end":71.3,"text":"So after you say \"Hello,\" look at the shoes near the door."},{"start":72.06,"end":75.94,"text":"If you are not sure, ask a simple question: \"Should I take off my shoes?\""},{"start":76.66,"end":79.64,"text":"A short question is polite, and it is better than guessing."},{"start":80.6,"end":83.46,"text":"So a greeting is not one fixed rule for a whole country."},{"start":83.96,"end":89.26,"text":"It is more like choosing clothes: you dress one way for work and another way for a family party."},{"start":90,"end":94.26,"text":"When you know the relationship and the situation, you can usually choose the right greeting."},{"start":94.26,"end":97.08,"text":"Picture a visitor in a small Paris shop."},{"start":97.68,"end":101.38,"text":"She walks in and asks at once, \"Excuse me, where is the bread?\""},{"start":102.08,"end":103.18,"text":"Her words are polite."},{"start":103.9,"end":106.32,"text":"But the shop worker may answer a little coldly."},{"start":106.84,"end":107.24,"text":"Why?"},{"start":107.7,"end":112.96,"text":"In France, it is important to say \"Bonjour\" before any request in a shop or café."},{"start":112.96,"end":115.28,"text":"In the evening, people say \"Bonsoir.\""},{"start":116.04,"end":120.5,"text":"Many people add \"Monsieur\" or \"Madame,\" for example \"Bonjour, Madame.\""},{"start":121.14,"end":122.42,"text":"This greeting is not extra."},{"start":122.7,"end":124.2,"text":"It is the first step of the request."},{"start":125.26,"end":129.84,"text":"Without it, the question can feel like an order, as if you spoke to a machine and not to a person."},{"start":130.8,"end":132.76,"text":"The same respect appears in other words."},{"start":133.62,"end":135.22,"text":"French has two words for \"you.\""},{"start":136.04,"end":139.12,"text":"People use the polite vous with strangers and at work."},{"start":139.58,"end":143.52,"text":"They change to the friendly tu only when the other person invites them."},{"start":143.52,"end":150.16,"text":"In formal situations or with older people, people use \"Madame\" or \"Monsieur\" and family names."},{"start":150.64,"end":152.96,"text":"They wait until they are invited to use first names."},{"start":153.88,"end":157.44,"text":"In the USA, \"Hello\" is also a normal and polite greeting."},{"start":157.92,"end":160.96,"text":"But in France, the greeting does a bigger job."},{"start":161.34,"end":164.9,"text":"It tells the other person, \"I see you before I ask you for something.\""},{"start":165.52,"end":168.34,"text":"So the visitor in the shop did not use bad words."},{"start":168.84,"end":171,"text":"She only skipped the step that comes first."},{"start":172.36,"end":173.5,"text":"Now imagine the end of a meal."},{"start":174.2,"end":176.44,"text":"In France and in the USA, you pay the bill."},{"start":177.04,"end":179.4,"text":"But what about the tip, the extra money for the waiter?"},{"start":180.26,"end":182.86,"text":"In the USA, a tip is expected in service jobs."},{"start":183.44,"end":187.38,"text":"For good service, people usually add 15–20% of the bill."},{"start":188.02,"end":192.62,"text":"That means about $1.50 to $2 for every $10 on the bill."},{"start":193.48,"end":195.18,"text":"Visitors sometimes see this as a gift."},{"start":195.74,"end":201.64,"text":"But many Americans see it as a normal part of the bill, so leaving nothing can send a strong message."},{"start":202.62,"end":204.14,"text":"In France, tipping is optional."},{"start":204.74,"end":209.62,"text":"People do not have to leave a tip, but waiters are happy to get one for excellent service."},{"start":210.36,"end":213.8,"text":"So the same action, leaving no tip, can mean very different things."},{"start":214.34,"end":215.4,"text":"In France it is normal."},{"start":215.86,"end":218.76,"text":"In the USA it can look like you were unhappy with the service."},{"start":219.32,"end":222.4,"text":"This is a good example of how respect moves to a different place."},{"start":222.93,"end":226.56,"text":"A French customer shows respect with \"Bonjour\" at the start."},{"start":227.12,"end":230.1,"text":"An American customer often shows it with money at the end."},{"start":231,"end":235.76,"text":"A visitor who brings only one country's habits can easily get one of these moments wrong."},{"start":237.3,"end":241.24,"text":"Think about two everyday places: a dinner table and a bus door."},{"start":242.04,"end":248.46,"text":"They look very different, but in both places the same question appears: do you go first, or do you wait for others?"},{"start":249.26,"end":254.98,"text":"At an American table, people usually wait until everyone has their food before they start eating."},{"start":255.78,"end":258.88,"text":"They put a napkin on their lap and chew with their mouth closed."},{"start":260.1,"end":262.86,"text":"They also stay at the table until everyone has finished."},{"start":262.86,"end":267.84,"text":"Starting early or leaving early shows that you are thinking only about yourself."},{"start":267.84,"end":271.34,"text":"Public transport follows the same idea."},{"start":272.04,"end":274.54,"text":"In the USA, cutting in line is rude."},{"start":275.36,"end":279.4,"text":"People usually wait their turn in a queue and when they get on a bus or train."},{"start":279.92,"end":283.06,"text":"In France, people also let passengers get off before they get on."},{"start":283.98,"end":287.68,"text":"They greet the bus driver when they get on, which is the \"Bonjour\" habit again."},{"start":288.46,"end":292.28,"text":"They give their seat to people who need it more, and they avoid loud noise."},{"start":293.32,"end":298.24,"text":"So the table and the bus share one rule: do not put yourself ahead of the group."},{"start":299.06,"end":302.06,"text":"The details change, but the idea stays the same."},{"start":303.04,"end":309.34,"text":"Wait for the others to be served, wait for people to get off, and keep your voice low so others can travel in peace."}],"words":[{"text":"Imagine","start":0,"end":0.44},{"text":"one","start":0.44,"end":0.74},{"text":"French","start":0.74,"end":1.02},{"text":"woman","start":1.02,"end":1.3},{"text":"on","start":1.3,"end":